WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.788
This five-step workflow is the foundation for the
entire module If you

00:00:04.812 --> 00:00:08.950
follow these steps aggregate queries become much
easier to design and debug

00:00:09.838 --> 00:00:15.362
Step one is ask What decision question are we
answering Maybe we

00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:19.050
want to know which sales categories generated the
most revenue or which

00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:24.512
diagnoses appear most often Step two is set the
grain This is

00:00:24.538 --> 00:00:29.862
the most important step Ask yourself what does one
output row represent

00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:36.300
One row per category One row per diagnosis One row
per

00:00:36.325 --> 00:00:40.412
department and month If that sentence is not clear
it is too

00:00:40.438 --> 00:00:46.112
early to write GROUP BY Step three is measure Decide
what calculation

00:00:46.138 --> 00:00:51.162
belongs in each row That could be COUNT SUM AVG MIN
MAX

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:56.112
or more than one of them Step four is filter Decide
which

00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:59.800
individual rows should be removed before grouping
and which completed groups should

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:04.825
be removed afterward That is where WHERE and HAVING
come in Step

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:10.650
five is validate Compare the summary with the
detailed data Check totals

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.512
row counts and a few example groups Notice that
syntax comes after

00:01:15.538 --> 00:01:20.288
reasoning A strong SQL habit is to define the
analytical meaning first

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:22.350
and then translate that meaning into SQL
